From 2a1265bb414dc4615df92f3a9bb7933e509552cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephan Raj Ignatious Durairaj <signatio@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 29 Jun 2026 22:06:51 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Traffic separation: Enable wireless Backhaul

Fix a set of bugs in the traffic separation (TS) subsystem covering
VLAN plumbing lifecycle, MLO interface naming, Multi-AP profile
propagation, and controller M2 TLV gating.

--- Problem 1: VLAN subinterfaces not recreated after agent reconnect ---

After an agent reconnect the WDS STA interface is torn down and
recreated by the kernel, destroying its VLAN subinterfaces.  Three
layers of the TS stack each had an early-exit that prevented
re-applying TS policies:

  * TrafficSeparationTask::handle_new_sta_iface() skipped WDS
    interfaces already present in m_tracked_wds_ifaces.
    Fix: remove the early-exit; always call add_trunk_port() for
    every current WDS interface.

  * TrafficSeparationManager::add_trunk_port() returned true
    immediately for any trunk already in m_trunk_port_plumbing_map
    without checking whether the VLAN subinterfaces were still alive.
    Fix: when TS is applied (is_applied()), call apply() on the
    existing plumbing object before returning so the subinterface
    existence check is triggered.

  * TrunkPortPlumbing8021q::apply() returned early when m_is_applied
    was true and the config was unchanged, even if the VLAN
    subinterfaces had been destroyed.
    Fix: before returning early, verify that m_private_subiface and
    m_guest_subiface still exist via network_utils::linux_iface_exists().
    If either is missing, fall through to clear() + re-apply.
    Also fix a secondary bug: the "Clearing and applying new policies"
    log was printed even on the unchanged-config early-return path;
    move it into the config-changed else branch.

--- Problem 2: MLO link suffix not stripped before WDS STA lookup ---

In an MLO deployment bss.iface_name may carry a per-link suffix
(e.g. wlan0-1).  get_all_sta_ifaces_for_bss() scans sysfs for WDS
STA interfaces whose parent matches the given name; with the suffix
present the scan returns nothing and no trunk ports are registered.

Fix: wrap bss.iface_name with net::strip_link_suffix() in both
collect_current_wds_ifaces() and collect_ports_from_db().

Same root cause in configuration_complete_wait_action(): the bridge
membership check called linux_iface_get_host_bridge(bssid.iface_name)
instead of linux_iface_get_host_bridge(base_iface_name), causing the
secondary-BSS skip logic to fail for MLO BSSes.
Fix: use base_iface_name.

--- Problem 3: Multi-AP profile and primary VLAN ID not propagated
               in nl80211 BWL ---

The nl80211 BWL left multi_ap_profile and multi_ap_primary_vlan_id
at zero in sACTION_BACKHAUL_CONNECTED_NOTIFICATION.  Two paths were
affected:

  * process_nl80211_event() (CTRL-EVENT-CONNECTED path): a comment
    said "not implemented on this flavour of BWL, leave it empty".
    Fix: parse multi_ap_profile and multi_ap_primary_vlanid from the
    event's key-value map.  Fields are optional; the code is
    backward-compatible with unpatched WPA supplicant builds.

  * reassociate() already-connected path: the synthesised Connected
    event was zero-initialised.
    Fix: populate the fields from connection_status, which is filled
    by read_status() before this code path is reached.

Add multi_ap_profile (uint8_t) and multi_ap_primary_vlanid (uint16_t)
to the private ConnectionStatus struct (sta_wlan_hal_nl80211.h) and
extract them from the WPA supplicant STATUS reply in read_status().

--- Problem 4: Guest VID hardcoded to compile-time default ---

build_ts_config() used bpl::DEFAULT_GUEST_VLAN_ID unconditionally
when no secondary VLAN IDs were present in the controller DB.
Platforms with a different operator-configured guest VID would use
the wrong VID for VLAN plumbing until the controller pushed a TS
policy.

Fix: read the platform-configured value via
bpl::cfg_get_traffic_separation_guest_vid() with fallback to the
compile-time default on failure.

--- Problem 5: WiFi trunk VLAN subinterfaces incorrectly persisted
               to /etc/config/network ---

persist_bridge_assignments() and reset_persistence() wrote WiFi WDS
trunk VLAN subinterfaces (e.g. wds0.<private_vid>) to
/etc/config/network.  WDS interfaces are ephemeral; persisting them
causes stale UCI entries after reboot and may cause the network
daemon to log errors.

Fix: exclude all WiFi trunk ports (!tp.is_ethernet) from both
persist_bridge_assignments() and reset_persistence().  Only Ethernet
trunk ports (persistent hardware interfaces) have their guest VLAN
subinterfaces written to UCI config.

--- Problem 6: TS TLVs added unconditionally to M2 message ---

In Controller::handle_cmdu_1905_autoconfiguration_WSC() the else
branch (agent not yet configured) unconditionally added
tlvProfile2TrafficSeparationPolicy and tlvProfile2Default802dotQSettings
to the M2 message even when is_custom_ts_enabled was false.  An agent
receiving TS TLVs with VID=0 would attempt incorrect VLAN plumbing.

Fix: guard both TLV additions with if (is_custom_ts_enabled).
Also correct the call order: add_profile_2default_802q_settings_tlv
is now called before add_traffic_separation_policy_tlv, matching the
Multi-AP spec ordering (default 802.1Q settings precede the policy TLV).

Signed-off-by: Stephan Raj Ignatious Durairaj <signatio@qti.qualcomm.com>
